The code statements that lead to the bug are:
WARN_ON(gc->cq_table[cq->queue.id]);
...
gc->cq_table[cq->queue.id] = gdma_cq;
Reason:
The `WARN_ON()` only logs a warning but does not prevent execution, so when a user supplies multiple WQs sharing the same CQ, the `gc->cq_table[cq->queue.id]` is already occupied. The subsequent assignment overwrites the existing entry, corrupting kernel data structures.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
if (gc->cq_table[cq->queue.id])
	return -EINVAL;
Reason:
This explicitly checks if the CQ table entry is already in use and returns an error (`-EINVAL`) without proceeding to overwrite it, thus rejecting the invalid configuration and preventing any corruption.

